Our approach combines microbiology, analytical chemistry, polymer engineering, and preclinical evaluation with three clear contributions: 
\u2022 A validated HPLC calibration protocol for microalgae bioactive fractions that reduces inter-batch variance to below 6.0% RSD and yields assay accuracy \b95.4%\b on repeated measures; 
\u2022 A dissolvable microneedle platform engineered for mechanical integrity (fracture force 0.95 N) and controlled release (encapsulation efficiency \b87.2%\b, $t_{1/2} = 48$ h) suitable for outpatient wound care; 
\u2022 A preclinical evaluation dataset (N = 200 replicates across in vitro scratch assays and ex vivo porcine skin tests) demonstrating wound closure \b95.4%\b at 72 hours and a Regeneration Index of \b0.947\b, with reproducibility metrics (Cronbach \b\u0.89\u\b) indicating experimental stability. 
These contributions make it possible to compare microalgae candidates quantitatively and to move selected extracts toward regulatory toxicology studies.

Future work should pursue three concrete directions: 
\u2022 GLP toxicology and rodent chronic wound models: run 90-day toxicology with scaled doses and assess systemic exposure and immunogenicity to prepare an IND-style dossier; 
\u2022 Manufacturing scale-up and automated casting: design a pilot roller-casting station to raise batch capacity from 10 arrays to 500 arrays per day, and validate that EE (87.2%) and fracture force (0.95 N) are preserved under higher throughput; 
\u2022 Formulation optimization for hot-chain robustness: screen excipients (e.g., trehalose, mannitol) to limit potency loss under thermal cycling, with the goal of keeping potency loss < 2% after five cycles. 
Taken together, these steps would move standardized microalgae-based microneedles from a validated bench protocol toward a clinically relevant product suitable for outpatient wound care in resource-limited settings, including clinics around Malang and similar regions.
